# What NOT to send to the Docker daemon as build context.
#
# `docker build .` does not read the current directory the way a compiler does.
# It tars up everything in it and ships that tarball to the daemon *before* the
# first instruction runs. So this file matters for three separate reasons:
#
# 1. Speed. Without it, every build would upload .venv/, the QM9 tarball and
#    the whole mlruns/ tree -- hundreds of megabytes, on every single build.
# 2. Cache correctness. `COPY . .` invalidates its layer whenever anything in
#    the context changes. If .git is in the context, a commit -- which changes
#    nothing about the code -- rebuilds the image from that layer down.
# 3. Secrets. Anything COPYed into an image stays in that layer forever, even
#    if a later instruction deletes it. `.env` never enters the context at all.
#
# The syntax is .gitignore-like but it is a *separate* mechanism: Docker does
# not read .gitignore, which is why untracked-but-present files such as .venv/
# have to be named again here.

# --- Version control ---
.git
.gitignore
.dockerignore

# --- Virtual environments (the image builds its own) ---
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- Secrets ---
.env

# --- Python build/test residue ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
build/
dist/

# --- Data and experiment output ---
# The dataset is training input; the image only serves. mlruns/ is a local
# tracking store measured in hundreds of megabytes.
data/
mlruns/
mlartifacts/
notebooks/

# --- Trained artifacts ---
# Everything in models/ except the one checkpoint the API serves. The negation
# has to come after the exclusion; Docker applies the last matching rule.
models/*
!models/served.pt

# --- Editor / OS / local notes ---
.idea/
.claude/
*.iml
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
progress.txt

# --- Docs and tests are not needed to run the service ---
# Kept out of the runtime image deliberately: a container should contain what
# it runs and nothing else. Note pyproject.toml requires README.md to build,
# so README.md is *not* excluded here.
tests/
Dockerfile
